/* Original revision notes mapped to the supplied first-assessment-2025 Physics guide. */
(() => {
const topics=[];
const add=(id,name,level,summary,sections,equations,worked,pitfalls,checklist)=>topics.push({id,name,theme:id[0],level,summary,sections:sections.map(([title,body])=>({title,body})),equations:equations.map(([tex,note])=>({tex,note})),worked:{question:worked[0],solution:worked[1]},pitfalls,checklist});
add('A.1','Kinematics','Core','Describe motion with vectors, graphs and a constant-acceleration model. Treat projectile motion as two linked one-dimensional problems.',[
['Position, distance and displacement',String.raw`Position locates an object relative to an origin. Displacement is the change in position, including direction; distance is the total length of its path. A runner completing a lap travels a non-zero distance but has zero displacement. Choose an axis and a positive direction before calculating. A negative displacement is a direction, not an indication that a calculation has failed.`],
['Speed, velocity and acceleration',String.raw`Average speed is total distance divided by elapsed time. Average velocity uses displacement instead. Instantaneous velocity describes motion at one moment and is the gradient of a position-time graph. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. A body can accelerate at constant speed when its direction changes. Negative acceleration only means acceleration in the chosen negative direction: it can describe speeding up or slowing down.`],
['Reading motion graphs',String.raw`On a displacement-time graph, the gradient gives velocity; a turning point has zero instantaneous velocity. On a velocity-time graph, the gradient is acceleration and signed area gives displacement. Add the magnitudes of areas above and below the axis for distance. On an acceleration-time graph, signed area gives change in velocity. A curved velocity-time graph has changing acceleration: use the gradient of a tangent for its instantaneous value.`],
['Uniformly accelerated motion',String.raw`The four kinematic equations apply only when acceleration is constant over the interval. List known quantities, select an equation that omits the unwanted variable, then substitute with consistent signs. An object released from rest has $u=0$, but an object thrown downward does not. Near Earth's surface, take $g=9.8\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ unless the question specifies otherwise. Free fall means gravity is the only significant force, including while an object rises.`],
['Projectiles',String.raw`Without air resistance, horizontal acceleration is zero and vertical acceleration is downward with magnitude $g$. Resolve launch velocity into $u_x=u\cos\theta$ and $u_y=u\sin\theta$. Horizontal and vertical motions share the same time. At the highest point, vertical velocity is zero but horizontal velocity usually remains non-zero. Find time from the vertical equation, then use horizontal displacement. Equal launch and landing heights permit a symmetric flight; unequal heights do not.`],
['When resistance matters',String.raw`Drag acts opposite the velocity through the fluid, so a real projectile is not generally symmetric or parabolic. Its horizontal speed decreases and its range is reduced. An object falling vertically reaches terminal speed when weight is balanced by drag and buoyancy: resultant force and acceleration are then zero. This does not mean that the forces vanish. The standard constant-acceleration equations cannot describe an entire fall while drag is changing.`]
],[[String.raw`v=u+at`,'Final velocity for constant acceleration.'],[String.raw`s=ut+\frac12at^2`,'Displacement after time t.'],[String.raw`v^2=u^2+2as`,'Useful when time is not given.'],[String.raw`s=\frac{u+v}{2}t`,'Average velocity is (u+v)/2 only for constant acceleration.']],['A ball leaves a 20.0 m high platform horizontally at 12.0 m/s. Neglect drag. How far horizontally does it travel?',String.raw`Choose downward positive for vertical motion: $20.0=\tfrac12(9.8)t^2$, so $t=2.02\,\mathrm{s}$. The horizontal speed is constant, hence $x=12.0(2.02)=24.2\,\mathrm{m}$. The horizontal velocity has no role in the falling time.`],['Do not use distance when a signed displacement is required.','A zero velocity at one instant does not imply zero acceleration.','Projectile range shortcuts require the launch and landing heights to match.'],['Interpret gradients and signed areas.','Resolve a projectile into components.','State the assumptions behind constant acceleration.']);
add('A.2','Forces and momentum','Core','Use interactions and conservation laws to explain acceleration, collisions and circular motion.',[
['Build the free-body diagram',String.raw`Choose one body and draw only forces acting on that body. Label weight, normal force, tension, friction, drag or other interactions. Components are mathematical parts of a force; do not count both a force and its components. Resolve forces along useful axes and calculate the vector resultant. A normal force is perpendicular to a surface and need not equal weight: an incline, extra load or vertical acceleration can change it.`],
['Newton’s laws',String.raw`A body maintains constant velocity when the resultant external force is zero. For constant mass, the resultant force equals $ma$, so acceleration follows the resultant rather than necessarily the direction of motion. Newton's third-law forces are equal and opposite forces of the same interaction acting on different bodies. They do not cancel on one body's free-body diagram. Constant speed in a straight line requires zero resultant force, whereas constant circular speed does not.`],
['Contact and fluid forces',String.raw`Static friction adjusts up to its limiting value $\mu_sF_N$; it is not always equal to that limit. Sliding friction is modelled by $\mu_dF_N$. A Hooke's-law spring exerts a restoring force opposite its extension from equilibrium. Stokes' law models slow, laminar flow around a small sphere. Buoyancy equals the weight of displaced fluid; use displaced fluid volume and fluid density. At terminal speed, buoyancy and drag together balance weight.`],
['Momentum and impulse',String.raw`Momentum is a vector. Impulse is the change in momentum and is the area under a resultant-force-time graph. A longer stopping interval reduces the average force for a given momentum change, explaining crumple zones. The form $F=\Delta p/\Delta t$ is more general than $F=ma$. When mass changes, define the system carefully and include momentum carried into or out of it rather than applying constant-mass reasoning blindly.`],
['Collisions and explosions',String.raw`Total momentum is conserved when external impulse on the system is negligible. Kinetic energy is also conserved in an elastic collision; it decreases in an inelastic collision, with energy transferred to internal energy, sound or deformation. Objects sticking together is a completely inelastic case. Explosions can increase kinetic energy by releasing stored internal energy. At HL, conserve momentum independently along two perpendicular axes for two-dimensional events.`],
['Circular motion',String.raw`A circular path requires inward acceleration $v^2/r$. The phrase centripetal force names the inward resultant, not an extra force to add to the diagram. Tension, friction, gravity or a combination can provide it. For a vertical circle, write a radial force equation separately at the top and bottom; weight changes its contribution relative to the inward direction. A radial force does no work instantaneously because it is perpendicular to velocity.`]
],[[String.raw`F=ma=\frac{\Delta p}{\Delta t}`,'Use the resultant force.'],[String.raw`p=mv,\qquad J=F\Delta t=\Delta p`,'Impulse uses average force if force varies.'],[String.raw`a=\frac{v^2}{r}=\omega^2r`,'Acceleration is radially inward.'],[String.raw`F_b=\rho Vg,\qquad F_d=6\pi\eta rv`,'Buoyancy and Stokes drag.']],['A 0.20 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m/s sticks to a stationary 0.40 kg trolley. Find their final speed.',String.raw`The system has initial momentum $0.20(3.0)=0.60\,\mathrm{kg\,m\,s^{-1}}$. Conservation gives $0.60=(0.20+0.40)v$, hence $v=1.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Kinetic energy falls from 0.90 J to 0.30 J, confirming that this collision is inelastic.`],['Third-law force pairs act on different bodies.','Centripetal force is a resultant, not an additional interaction.','Static friction can be smaller than its maximum.'],['Draw and resolve a free-body diagram.','Apply impulse and vector momentum conservation.','Identify the force that supplies radial acceleration.']);
add('A.3','Work, energy and power','Core','Track energy stores and transfers, use work to connect forces with energy changes, and distinguish energy from its transfer rate.',[
['Choose a system',String.raw`Energy is conserved across all stores and transfers, but the mechanical energy of a chosen system need not remain constant. State the system boundary and identify transfers by work, heating or radiation. A Sankey diagram shows the input divided between useful and other outputs; its widths represent energy or power consistently. Dissipated energy remains in the surroundings, commonly as internal energy, although it becomes less useful for the intended task.`],
['Work and force',String.raw`Work by a constant force is the component of force along displacement multiplied by displacement. Positive work transfers energy to kinetic motion when the force has a forward component; negative work can reduce kinetic energy. A perpendicular force does zero work. For a changing force, the area under a force-displacement graph gives work. The work done by the resultant force equals the change in kinetic energy, while individual forces can transfer energy between different stores.`],
['Mechanical energy stores',String.raw`Translational kinetic energy depends on speed squared and may also be expressed using momentum. Gravitational potential energy change near a planet's surface is $mg\Delta h$ when the field is approximately uniform. Elastic potential energy is the triangular area under a Hooke's-law force-extension graph. The factor of one half appears because spring force increases from zero as extension increases. Potential-energy zero levels are chosen references; energy differences determine observable transfers.`],
['Conservation as a method',String.raw`When resistive forces are negligible, add kinetic, gravitational and elastic potential energies at two positions and equate the totals. This can find speed without knowing time or the detailed path. If friction acts, include the energy transferred to internal energy. For a sliding object, work by friction is negative along the path. Do not assume that energy conservation alone provides a direction; combine it with the geometry and the allowed motion.`],
['Power and efficiency',String.raw`Power is energy transferred per unit time. A motor can deliver the same energy more quickly by operating at higher power. For force parallel to velocity, instantaneous power is $Fv$; for a non-parallel force, use the component along motion. Efficiency compares useful output with total input, using either energies over the same interval or powers. Quote a fraction or multiply by 100 for a percentage. An efficiency greater than 100% signals a mistaken system boundary or calculation.`],
['Fuel and performance',String.raw`Fuel energy density is energy released per unit mass, usually in joules per kilogram. Available energy equals fuel mass multiplied by energy density, but useful energy also depends on conversion efficiency. A more energy-dense fuel does not automatically provide greater power: power depends on how quickly it is consumed. In comparisons, check whether values refer to useful output, total chemical input or losses, and keep the same time interval and units.`]
],[[String.raw`W=Fs\cos\theta`,'Constant force, with angle between force and displacement.'],[String.raw`E_k=\frac12mv^2=\frac{p^2}{2m}`,'Translational kinetic energy.'],[String.raw`\Delta E_p=mg\Delta h,\qquad E_H=\frac12k(\Delta x)^2`,'Uniform-gravity and spring stores.'],[String.raw`P=\frac{\Delta W}{\Delta t}=Fv,\qquad \eta=\frac{E_{\rm out}}{E_{\rm in}}`,'The Fv form assumes parallel force and velocity.']],['A 60 kg person climbs 4.0 m in 8.0 s. Estimate useful mechanical power.',String.raw`The useful energy gain is $mg\Delta h=60(9.8)(4.0)=2352\,\mathrm{J}$. Divide by time: $P=2352/8.0=294\,\mathrm{W}$. The chemical power consumed by the person must be larger because the process is not perfectly efficient.`],['Work uses displacement along the force, not total path in every situation.','Mechanical energy can decrease while total energy is conserved.','Power is measured in watts, not joules.'],['Select an energy system and draw transfers.','Use graph area to calculate work.','Calculate power, efficiency and fuel requirements.']);
add('A.4','Rigid body mechanics','HL','Extend motion and conservation ideas to rotating extended bodies, including rolling without slipping.',[
['Torque and equilibrium',String.raw`Torque describes the turning effect of a force about a specified axis. Its magnitude is force multiplied by perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action. In $Fr\sin\theta$, the angle is between the radius vector and force. Assign opposite signs to clockwise and counter-clockwise torques. Rotational equilibrium requires zero resultant torque. Complete static equilibrium also requires zero resultant force: these are separate conditions and both must be checked.`],
['Angular kinematics',String.raw`Angular displacement is measured in radians, angular velocity is its rate of change, and angular acceleration is the rate of change of angular velocity. With constant angular acceleration, use the rotational equivalents of the linear kinematic equations. Keep angular and linear quantities distinct. Points on a rigid rotating body share angular velocity, but their tangential speeds increase with distance from the axis through $v=r\omega$. The same reasoning gives tangential acceleration $r\alpha$.`],
['Moment of inertia',String.raw`Moment of inertia measures resistance to angular acceleration about a chosen axis. It depends on mass distribution, not simply total mass. For point masses, add each contribution $mr^2$; moving a mass twice as far from the axis multiplies its contribution by four. A formula for an extended shape must correspond to the correct axis. The syllabus supplies specific shape formulas when needed, so focus on interpreting their physical dependence.`],
['Rotational dynamics and energy',String.raw`For a fixed moment of inertia, resultant torque equals $I\alpha$. Rotational kinetic energy is $\tfrac12I\omega^2$. An object rolling without slipping has both translational kinetic energy of its centre of mass and rotational kinetic energy about that centre. Use $v=r\omega$ only for no-slip rolling. On an ideal rigid stationary surface, static friction can enable rolling without dissipating mechanical energy; the contact point is instantaneously at rest.`],
['Angular momentum',String.raw`For rotation about the chosen axis, angular momentum is $I\omega$. It stays constant when the resultant external torque is zero, even if moment of inertia changes. A skater pulling in their arms reduces $I$ and increases angular speed. Rotational kinetic energy can increase at the same time because internal work is done. Conservation of angular momentum does not automatically imply conservation of kinetic energy.`],
['Coupled bodies and angular impulse',String.raw`Angular impulse equals the change in angular momentum and is the area under a torque-time graph. When rotating bodies couple together, apply angular momentum conservation if external torque is negligible and then use their combined moment of inertia. They settle to a common angular speed, generally with less rotational kinetic energy. Compare initial and final energies after finding the speed; the missing mechanical energy becomes internal energy through friction during coupling.`]
],[[String.raw`\tau=Fr\sin\theta,\qquad \tau=I\alpha`,'Use the resultant torque and specified axis.'],[String.raw`I=\sum mr^2,\qquad L=I\omega`,'Moment of inertia and angular momentum.'],[String.raw`E_k=\frac12I\omega^2=\frac{L^2}{2I}`,'Rotational kinetic energy.'],[String.raw`\omega_f^2=\omega_i^2+2\alpha\Delta\theta`,'Constant angular acceleration only.']],['A rotating platform has I = 4.0 kg m² and angular speed 3.0 rad/s. Its moment of inertia increases to 6.0 kg m² with no external torque. Find the final angular speed.',String.raw`Conserve angular momentum: $4.0(3.0)=6.0\omega_f$, giving $\omega_f=2.0\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$. The kinetic energy changes from $\tfrac12(4.0)(3.0)^2=18\,\mathrm{J}$ to $\tfrac12(6.0)(2.0)^2=12\,\mathrm{J}$. Angular momentum is conserved although rotational kinetic energy is not.`],['Torque uses a perpendicular lever arm.','Specify the axis when using a moment of inertia.','Rolling objects usually have two kinetic-energy contributions.'],['Apply both equilibrium conditions.','Use angular kinematics and dynamics.','Distinguish angular-momentum and energy conservation.']);
add('A.5','Galilean and special relativity','HL','Compare measurements in inertial frames and use invariant light speed to understand time dilation, length contraction and simultaneity.',[
['Frames and events',String.raw`An event is something occurring at a particular place and time. A reference frame assigns coordinates to it. An inertial frame is non-accelerating, and Newton's laws have the same form in all such frames. In a Galilean transformation for a frame moving at speed $v$ along the positive x-axis, $x'=x-vt$ and $t'=t$. This treats time as universal and works well at everyday speeds.`],
['The relativistic postulates',String.raw`Special relativity states that the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames and that light in vacuum has the same speed for all inertial observers. Ordinary velocity subtraction cannot preserve this speed. The Lorentz factor $\gamma$ measures the size of relativistic effects: it approaches 1 when $v\ll c$ and grows without limit as $v$ approaches $c$. Apply Lorentz transformations to complete events rather than transforming a distance while ignoring its associated times.`],
['Proper time and time dilation',String.raw`Proper time is the interval measured in the frame where the two events occur at the same position, such as two ticks of a clock carried by one observer. Another inertial observer measures a longer interval, $\Delta t=\gamma\Delta t_0$. Identify the events before choosing the proper interval. Fast atmospheric muons survive to reach Earth's surface in larger numbers than non-relativistic lifetime estimates predict; their time-dilated lifetimes explain the observation in Earth's frame.`],
['Proper length and simultaneity',String.raw`Proper length is measured in an object's rest frame. A moving observer measures a shorter length parallel to relative motion. Measuring length means recording both endpoint positions simultaneously in the measuring observer's frame. Events simultaneous in one frame need not be simultaneous in another when separated in space. Length contraction and time dilation therefore cannot be mixed casually as if all observers agreed on which distant events happen together.`],
['Velocity transformation and interval',String.raw`Relativistic velocity addition changes the denominator as well as subtracting frame speed in the numerator. Substituting a light signal still produces $c$, unlike the Galilean equation. The spacetime interval $(c\Delta t)^2-(\Delta x)^2$ is invariant between inertial frames. If it is positive, a frame exists in which the two events occur at the same place. A zero interval describes events connected by light.`],
['Spacetime diagrams',String.raw`Use x horizontally and ct vertically, with equal plotted units when interpreting slopes. A stationary body's world line is vertical. A constant-speed body's world line is straight, and light has a 45-degree line on equally scaled axes. The angle from the time axis satisfies $\tan\theta=v/c$. A physical massive particle's world line lies inside the light cone. On diagrams showing primed axes, their physical unit scales must be established using invariant intervals rather than assumed equal by visual length.`]
],[[String.raw`\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\frac{v^2}{c^2}}}`,'Lorentz factor for relative speed v.'],[String.raw`x'=\gamma(x-vt),\qquad t'=\gamma\left(t-\frac{vx}{c^2}\right)`,'Standard configuration with coincident origins at t = 0.'],[String.raw`\Delta t=\gamma\Delta t_0,\qquad L=\frac{L_0}{\gamma}`,'Identify proper quantities first.'],[String.raw`u'=\frac{u-v}{1-\frac{uv}{c^2}}`,'Collinear velocity transformation.']],['A spacecraft moves at 0.80c. A clock aboard records 6.0 μs between two ticks. What time interval is measured in the Earth frame?',String.raw`The ticks occur at one position in the spacecraft frame, so 6.0 μs is proper time. $\gamma=1/\sqrt{1-0.80^2}=1.667$. Thus $\Delta t=1.667(6.0)=10.0\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ in the Earth frame.`],['Proper time belongs to events at the same position.','Length contraction applies parallel to motion.','A spacetime diagram uses ct, not an unscaled time axis, for light-cone angles.'],['Identify a proper interval or length.','Transform event coordinates and velocities.','Interpret world lines and invariant intervals.']);
add('B.1','Thermal energy transfers','Core','Link thermal behaviour to particle motion, then quantify heating, phase changes, conduction and thermal radiation.',[
['Particles and internal energy',String.raw`A solid has particles vibrating about relatively fixed positions; liquid particles remain close but can rearrange; gas particles move far apart and approximately freely between collisions. Internal energy includes random kinetic energy and intermolecular potential energy. Temperature indicates the average random kinetic energy rather than the total internal energy. A large cold body may therefore store more internal energy than a small hot body. The mean translational kinetic energy relation applies directly to ideal-gas particles.`],
['Temperature and thermal equilibrium',String.raw`Net thermal energy transfers spontaneously from higher temperature to lower temperature. At thermal equilibrium there is no net transfer between the bodies, although microscopic energy exchange continues. Kelvin is the absolute scale required in radiation and gas laws. A temperature change has the same numerical value in kelvin and degrees Celsius, but absolute temperatures do not. The booklet uses the approximation $T/\mathrm{K}=\theta/{}^\circ\mathrm{C}+273$.`],
['Heating and changing phase',String.raw`For a temperature rise without a change of phase, use $Q=mc\Delta T$. Specific heat capacity is a material property that may depend on temperature; questions commonly treat it as constant. During a phase change, $Q=mL$ and temperature stays constant in the ideal model because energy changes particle arrangement and potential energy. Heating ice to steam requires separate stages for warming and phase changes. Include the container's heat capacity when significant.`],
['Conduction and convection',String.raw`Conduction transfers energy through microscopic interactions without bulk movement of matter; mobile electrons also contribute in metals. Its steady transfer rate increases with area, thermal conductivity and temperature difference and decreases with thickness. Convection involves bulk fluid motion caused by density differences, commonly when warm fluid expands and rises under gravity. Insulating materials limit conduction, trapped gas limits convection, and reflective surfaces can reduce radiative transfer.`],
['Black-body radiation',String.raw`All bodies emit electromagnetic radiation. A black body is an ideal absorber and emitter. Its total emitted power is $\sigma AT^4$, so doubling absolute temperature multiplies emitted power by sixteen. Real surfaces radiate a fraction given by emissivity. Net radiative loss also depends on energy absorbed from surroundings; emitted power alone is not automatically net loss. A black-body spectrum has a broad distribution of wavelengths rather than a single emitted colour.`],
['Stars and inverse-square spreading',String.raw`Wien's displacement law connects the spectrum's peak wavelength with surface temperature: hotter bodies peak at shorter wavelengths. Luminosity is total emitted power; apparent brightness is power received per unit area at a distance. With isotropic emission and negligible absorption, brightness follows an inverse-square law because the same power spreads across a sphere. A dim-looking star might be intrinsically faint or simply far away; brightness alone does not determine its luminosity.`]
],[[String.raw`Q=mc\Delta T,\qquad Q=mL`,'Choose the equation appropriate to the stage.'],[String.raw`\frac{\Delta Q}{\Delta t}=kA\frac{\Delta T}{\Delta x}`,'Magnitude of steady conductive transfer.'],[String.raw`L=\sigma AT^4,\qquad b=\frac{L}{4\pi d^2}`,'Black-body luminosity and apparent brightness.'],[String.raw`\lambda_{\max}T=2.9\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m\,K}`,'Wien displacement law.']],['How much energy heats 0.50 kg of water through 20 K if c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹?',String.raw`There is no phase change, so $Q=mc\Delta T=0.50(4200)(20)=4.2\times10^4\,\mathrm{J}$. A 200 W heater would require at least $Q/P=210\,\mathrm{s}$; losses and heating the container make the real time longer.`],['Use kelvin for powers of absolute temperature.','Heat is an energy transfer, not a substance stored inside a body.','A phase change can absorb energy without raising temperature.'],['Model a multistage heating process.','Compare conduction, convection and radiation.','Use spectra and brightness to infer stellar properties.']);
add('B.2','Greenhouse effect','Core','Use radiation and energy balance to model a planet and explain how its atmosphere changes surface temperature.',[
['Incident sunlight and geometry',String.raw`The solar constant is the solar power per unit area received on a surface perpendicular to the incoming rays at a specified distance from the Sun. A spherical planet intercepts radiation over a disc of area $\pi R^2$ but distributes energy over its full surface area $4\pi R^2$. This gives a mean incident flux $S/4$ before accounting for reflection. It is a geometrical average, not a claim that every point receives sunlight continuously.`],
['Albedo and absorbed power',String.raw`Albedo is the fraction of incident power scattered back rather than absorbed. The absorbed average flux is therefore $(1-a)S/4$. Snow, ice and many clouds have high albedo; darker surfaces generally have lower albedo. Planetary albedo varies with surface cover, clouds, latitude and time. If albedo increases while other model parameters stay fixed, less incoming energy is absorbed and the equilibrium temperature decreases.`],
['Emissivity and outgoing radiation',String.raw`Emissivity compares a surface's radiation with a black body at the same temperature. Its total emitted power per unit area is $\varepsilon\sigma T^4$ in a simple grey-body model. A low emissivity means less power emitted at a given temperature, so a larger temperature may be needed to balance the same absorbed input. Emissivity and albedo refer to different processes and wavelength ranges; they should not be treated as interchangeable numbers.`],
['Equilibrium temperature',String.raw`At radiative equilibrium, average absorbed power equals average power leaving the chosen system. For an atmosphere-free grey sphere, $(1-a)S/4=\varepsilon\sigma T^4$. Solve for temperature with a fourth root and use kelvin. A temporary imbalance changes internal energy and temperature. Equilibrium means equal rates of energy input and output, not absence of energy flow. Simple models omit spatial variation, oceans, clouds and many wavelength-dependent effects.`],
['The greenhouse mechanism',String.raw`A relatively warm planetary surface emits mostly infrared radiation. Water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide absorb particular infrared wavelengths through transitions involving molecular energy levels. Molecular vibrations can also be described using a resonance model. The gases subsequently emit radiation in all directions, including toward the surface. Surface energy balance therefore includes absorbed sunlight and downward atmospheric radiation. Energy still ultimately escapes to space.`],
['Enhanced effect and coupled models',String.raw`Human activities increase concentrations of some greenhouse gases, for example carbon dioxide from fossil-fuel combustion and methane from agriculture and fuel systems. This changes radiative exchange and can raise the equilibrium surface temperature. In a two-layer model, write separate balance equations for surface and atmosphere: an atmospheric layer can radiate both upward and downward. Do not equate surface emission with emission to space when an absorbing atmosphere intervenes. Feedbacks such as ice-albedo changes make real climate response more complex than a single-layer calculation.`]
],[[String.raw`a=\frac{P_{\rm scattered}}{P_{\rm incident}}`,'Albedo is dimensionless.'],[String.raw`\varepsilon=\frac{\frac{P}{A}}{\sigma T^4}`,'Emissivity relative to a black body at the same T.'],[String.raw`\frac{(1-a)S}{4}=\varepsilon\sigma T^4`,'Equilibrium model without explicit atmospheric layers.']],['A planet has S = 1360 W/m² and albedo 0.30. Calculate its globally averaged absorbed solar flux.',String.raw`The average incident flux is $1360/4=340\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. The absorbed fraction is $1-0.30=0.70$, so the absorbed flux is $0.70(340)=238\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. This can be balanced against outgoing radiation for the planet-atmosphere system.`],['The factor of four comes from disc area versus sphere area.','Greenhouse gases do not create energy.','Separate surface balance from whole-planet balance.'],['Set up a planetary energy balance.','Distinguish albedo from emissivity.','Explain infrared absorption and atmospheric re-emission.']);
add('B.3','Gas laws','Core','Connect pressure, volume and temperature to the kinetic behaviour of an ideal gas.',[
['Pressure from collisions',String.raw`Pressure is the normal force per unit area. Gas molecules collide with container walls and change momentum; the corresponding rate of momentum transfer produces a force on the walls. Increasing collision frequency or momentum change increases pressure. Gas pressure acts in all directions in equilibrium because molecular motion is random. Use absolute pressure rather than a gauge pressure when applying gas laws unless a pressure difference is explicitly required.`],
['The ideal-gas model',String.raw`Ideal particles have negligible volume compared with their container, no intermolecular forces except during collisions, random motion and elastic collisions. Real gases approximate this model best at high temperature and low density or pressure, away from condensation. At low temperature, attractions matter; at high density, particle volume matters. The model is a useful approximation rather than a claim that all real gases behave ideally in every condition.`],
['Moles and molecules',String.raw`The amount in moles is the number of particles divided by the Avogadro constant. Use $PV=nRT$ with moles or $PV=Nk_BT$ with particle number. The two forms are equivalent because $R=N_Ak_B$. Do not insert number of molecules into the molar equation. Convert cubic centimetres or litres to cubic metres, Celsius to kelvin and pressure to pascals before using SI constants.`],
['Special processes',String.raw`For a fixed amount of ideal gas, $PV/T$ remains constant between equilibrium states. At fixed temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume. At fixed volume, pressure is proportional to absolute temperature. At fixed pressure, volume is proportional to absolute temperature. These statements need the fixed quantity explicitly identified. A pressure-volume diagram represents states; an isothermal curve is a rectangular hyperbola, while an isobaric change is horizontal.`],
['Kinetic energy and speed',String.raw`Average translational kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature. Molecules do not all travel at one speed: use the mean-square speed in the kinetic-pressure equation. Its square root is the root-mean-square speed, not the arithmetic mean speed. At the same temperature, different ideal gases have equal mean translational kinetic energy per molecule, but lighter molecules have greater rms speed. Random directions can give zero mean velocity despite non-zero mean-square speed.`],
['Internal energy',String.raw`For a monatomic ideal gas, internal energy is the sum of random translational kinetic energies and depends only on temperature and amount of gas. There is no intermolecular potential-energy contribution in the ideal model. If temperature remains constant, internal energy remains constant even while pressure and volume change. The expression with a factor of three halves is specific to a monatomic ideal gas; molecules with additional active degrees of freedom require a different treatment.`]
],[[String.raw`PV=nRT=Nk_BT`,'Equivalent molar and particle forms.'],[String.raw`P=\frac13\rho\overline{v^2}`,'Use mean-square molecular speed.'],[String.raw`U=\frac32nRT=\frac32Nk_BT`,'Monatomic ideal gas only.'],[String.raw`n=\frac{N}{N_A}`,'Particle number converted to amount of substance.']],['A fixed amount of gas occupies 2.0 L at 300 K. What volume does it occupy at 450 K at the same pressure?',String.raw`At constant pressure, $V/T$ is constant. Thus $V_2=V_1T_2/T_1=2.0(450/300)=3.0\,\mathrm{L}$. A ratio calculation permits the same volume unit on both sides; direct use of $PV=nRT$ requires cubic metres.`],['Use kelvin and absolute pressure.','Mean-square speed is not the square of mean velocity.','The monatomic internal-energy formula is not universal for all gases.'],['State and evaluate ideal-gas assumptions.','Move between molar and particle descriptions.','Interpret constant-pressure, -volume and -temperature changes.']);
add('B.4','Thermodynamics','HL','Apply conservation of energy with a consistent sign convention and use entropy to distinguish possible processes from impossible ones.',[
['System boundaries and signs',String.raw`A closed system exchanges energy but not matter with its surroundings. An isolated system exchanges neither. Use the booklet convention $Q=\Delta U+W$: Q is net thermal energy supplied to the system, and W is work done by the system. Expansion gives positive W; compression gives negative W. Heating gives positive Q. Establishing these signs before substituting is more reliable than trying to remember a separate rearrangement for each process.`],
['Work on a pressure-volume diagram',String.raw`For constant pressure, work done by a gas is $P\Delta V$. More generally, the signed area under a pressure-volume path gives work. Expansion moves right and produces positive work; compression moves left and produces negative work. A complete cycle returns to the same thermodynamic state, so the change in internal energy is zero. The enclosed area is the net work, positive for a clockwise engine cycle.`],
['Four useful processes',String.raw`At constant volume, W is zero and supplied heat increases internal energy. At constant pressure, heat supplies both internal-energy change and expansion work. For a monatomic ideal gas in an isothermal process, internal energy is unchanged, so Q equals W. An adiabatic process transfers no heat, so expansion work lowers internal energy and temperature. Adiabatic does not mean constant temperature. A monatomic ideal gas follows $PV^{5/3}=\text{constant}$ for the reversible adiabatic model.`],
['Entropy and microstates',String.raw`Entropy relates to the number of microscopic arrangements compatible with a macroscopic state. $S=k_B\ln\Omega$ associates more possible microstates with greater entropy. Energy spreading is a useful interpretation, while simply calling entropy disorder can be imprecise. For a reversible heat transfer at constant temperature, $\Delta S=Q_{\rm rev}/T$. For a temperature-changing body, do not divide an arbitrary total heat by one chosen temperature without a justified approximation.`],
['The second law',String.raw`The entropy of an isolated system cannot decrease; it stays constant in a reversible idealization and increases in an irreversible process. A local entropy decrease is possible if the surroundings gain at least as much. The Clausius statement excludes spontaneous net heat flow from cold to hot without another effect. The Kelvin statement excludes a cyclic engine whose only result is converting heat drawn from one reservoir entirely into work.`],
['Heat engines and Carnot limit',String.raw`A heat engine absorbs energy from a hot reservoir, produces net work and rejects energy to a cold reservoir. Its efficiency is net useful work divided by heat drawn from the hot reservoir. A reversible Carnot engine sets the upper efficiency at two reservoir temperatures. Both temperatures must be in kelvin. Reaching 100% efficiency would require a zero-kelvin cold reservoir in this model; real engines also incur irreversible losses.`]
],[[String.raw`Q=\Delta U+W,\qquad W=P\Delta V`,'W is positive for work done by the system.'],[String.raw`\Delta U=\frac32nR\Delta T`,'Monatomic ideal gas.'],[String.raw`\Delta S=\frac{Q_{\rm rev}}{T},\qquad S=k_B\ln\Omega`,'The heat-over-temperature expression needs reversible isothermal transfer.'],[String.raw`\eta_{\rm Carnot}=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}`,'Maximum efficiency between two reservoir temperatures.']],['A gas receives 800 J of thermal energy while doing 300 J of work. Find its internal-energy change.',String.raw`Use $Q=\Delta U+W$. Both heat input and expansion work are positive, so $\Delta U=800-300=500\,\mathrm{J}$. Some input energy remains as internal energy and the rest crosses the boundary as work.`],['Adiabatic and isothermal are different conditions.','The area under a p-V graph is work, not change in internal energy.','Entropy may decrease locally while total entropy increases.'],['Apply the first law with explicit signs.','Interpret cycles and process paths.','Use entropy and the Carnot limit.']);
add('B.5','Current and circuits','Core','Model charge flow, energy transfer and resistance in dc circuits, including sources with internal resistance.',[
['Charge flow and potential difference',String.raw`Electric current is the rate of charge flow through a cross-section. Conventional current follows the motion that positive charge would have; electron drift in a metal is opposite. Charge is conserved at a junction. Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge between two points. A cell's emf is energy supplied per unit charge by its non-electrical source, such as a chemical reaction or absorbed radiation in a solar cell.`],
['Resistance and resistivity',String.raw`Resistance is the ratio V/I at a particular operating point. Ohm's law states that current is proportional to potential difference when physical conditions, especially temperature, are constant. Resistivity is a material property; wire resistance also depends on length and area. A metal's resistance usually rises as it warms because lattice vibrations increase scattering. A filament lamp is non-ohmic because its temperature changes significantly as current increases.`],
['Series and parallel',String.raw`In series, components carry the same current and their potential differences add. Their resistances add because the same charge must transfer energy in every component. Parallel branches share the same potential difference, while current divides at junctions. Reciprocal resistances add in parallel, so the equivalent resistance is smaller than the smallest branch resistance. Redraw complicated networks around junctions rather than judging their topology from the physical positions on a diagram.`],
['Energy and power',String.raw`Electrical power is IV. Combining this with resistance gives $I^2R$ and $V^2/R$. Which form is useful depends on what stays constant: at constant current, larger R gives greater power; at constant voltage, larger R gives smaller power. Thermal energy produced over time is Pt when power is constant. Current is not used up by a resistor; charge continues around the circuit while energy is transferred to the surroundings.`],
['Real cells and meters',String.raw`A cell with emf $\varepsilon$ and internal resistance r has terminal potential difference $V=\varepsilon-Ir$ while delivering current. Internal resistance dissipates power $I^2r$. An ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance and connects in parallel; an ideal ammeter has zero resistance and connects in series. Non-ideal meters affect the circuit, so include their stated resistances. A terminal-voltage-versus-current graph has intercept emf and gradient minus internal resistance.`],
['Sensors and potential dividers',String.raw`A potential divider shares an input voltage between series resistances. The output across one resistor is its fraction of the total resistance multiplied by the supply voltage, provided loading is negligible. A typical NTC thermistor decreases resistance as temperature increases, and an LDR decreases resistance with increasing light intensity. Whether output voltage rises or falls depends on which component the output is measured across. A potentiometer allows a continuously adjustable division.`]
],[[String.raw`I=\frac{\Delta q}{\Delta t},\qquad V=\frac{W}{q}`,'Charge rate and energy per unit charge.'],[String.raw`R=\frac{V}{I},\qquad \rho=\frac{RA}{L}`,'Resistance and resistivity.'],[String.raw`P=IV=I^2R=\frac{V^2}{R}`,'Use consistent operating values.'],[String.raw`\varepsilon=I(R+r)`,'External load R and internal resistance r in series.']],['A 6.0 V emf cell with internal resistance 1.0 Ω drives a 5.0 Ω resistor. Find current and terminal voltage.',String.raw`The total resistance is $5.0+1.0=6.0\,\Omega$, so $I=6.0/6.0=1.0\,\mathrm{A}$. Terminal voltage is $IR=5.0\,\mathrm{V}$, or $6.0-1.0(1.0)$. The remaining 1.0 V corresponds to energy dissipated inside the cell per coulomb.`],['Current is conserved, not consumed.','Emf and terminal potential difference need not be equal.','An I-V gradient gives reciprocal resistance only with the axes specified.'],['Reduce resistor networks.','Explain ohmic and non-ohmic characteristics.','Analyse internal resistance and sensor dividers.']);
add('C.1','Simple harmonic motion','Core','Identify a restoring acceleration proportional to displacement, then connect phase, speed and energy in an oscillator. Quantitative phase and energy relations are HL extensions.',[
['The defining condition',String.raw`Simple harmonic motion occurs when acceleration is proportional to displacement from equilibrium and directed toward equilibrium. The minus sign in $a=-\omega^2x$ expresses the restoring direction. A periodic motion is not automatically SHM: a bouncing ball repeats but does not satisfy this equation. Choose the equilibrium position as x = 0. For a vertical mass-spring oscillator, the spring's static extension shifts the equilibrium position but does not change the ideal period.`],
['Describing a cycle',String.raw`Amplitude is the maximum displacement magnitude. Period is the time for one complete cycle, frequency is cycles per second, and angular frequency is $2\pi f$. At equilibrium, speed is maximum and acceleration is zero. At an extreme position, speed is zero and acceleration magnitude is maximum. Displacement and acceleration are opposite in sign. Sketch displacement, velocity and acceleration against time on aligned axes to identify these relationships.`],
['Mass-spring and pendulum models',String.raw`A Hooke's-law spring produces a restoring force proportional to displacement, giving a period proportional to the square root of mass and inversely proportional to the square root of spring constant. A simple pendulum approximates SHM for small angular displacements, when the restoring component is proportional to angle. Its period depends on length and local gravitational field strength, not mass. Large swings depart from the small-angle approximation.`],
['Phase and initial conditions — HL',String.raw`The phase angle tells where an oscillator is within its cycle. In $x=x_0\sin(\omega t+\phi)$, choose the phase constant to match both initial displacement and initial direction of motion. The corresponding cosine velocity expression is one quarter cycle ahead of displacement. All angles must be in radians when using angular frequency. A phase difference of $\pi$ means opposite displacements, while $2\pi$ describes equivalent positions within a cycle.`],
['Energy changes',String.raw`In an ideal undamped oscillator, total mechanical energy is constant. At the extremes it is entirely potential energy relative to the equilibrium zero, and at equilibrium it is entirely kinetic. At intermediate positions both stores contribute. At HL, potential energy is proportional to displacement squared and total energy to amplitude squared. Halving amplitude therefore quarters total energy. The negative or positive velocity branch must be chosen using the direction of motion.`],
['Using data and testing the model',String.raw`Time several cycles and divide by the number to reduce fractional timing uncertainty. For a mass-spring investigation, a plot of period squared against mass is linear in the ideal model. For a pendulum, period squared is proportional to length. Damping makes the amplitude decrease because energy leaves the oscillator; if damping is small, SHM formulas can approximate individual cycles. Check that the model's assumptions fit the actual apparatus before interpreting a gradient.`]
],[[String.raw`a=-\omega^2x,\qquad T=\frac1f=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}`,'Defining condition and cycle measures.'],[String.raw`T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{l}{g}}`,'Spring and small-angle pendulum periods.'],[String.raw`x=x_0\sin(\omega t+\phi),\quad v=\omega x_0\cos(\omega t+\phi)`,'HL: phase-resolved motion.'],[String.raw`E_T=\frac12m\omega^2x_0^2,\qquad E_p=\frac12m\omega^2x^2`,'HL: energy measured relative to equilibrium.']],['A 0.20 kg mass oscillates on a spring of spring constant 80 N/m. Find its period.',String.raw`Use $T=2\pi\sqrt{m/k}=2\pi\sqrt{0.20/80}=0.314\,\mathrm{s}$. Its angular frequency is $\sqrt{k/m}=20\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}$. Changing the amplitude does not change this period within the ideal Hooke's-law model.`],['Periodic motion does not necessarily satisfy SHM.','Use equilibrium displacement, not total spring length.','The pendulum formula assumes small angles.'],['Identify SHM from force or acceleration.','Compare motion and energy at characteristic positions.','HL: use phase and energy equations.']);
add('C.2','Wave model','Core','Describe travelling disturbances and distinguish particle motion from propagation of energy.',[
['What a wave transports',String.raw`A travelling wave transfers energy through a propagating disturbance. In a mechanical wave, neighbouring particles interact so the disturbance moves while particles oscillate about equilibrium; there is no required net transport of the medium. A floating marker bobbing as a water wave passes illustrates the distinction. Wave speed describes propagation of a phase or disturbance, whereas particle velocity describes local motion. The two quantities are physically different and need not have equal magnitudes.`],
['Transverse and longitudinal waves',String.raw`In a transverse wave, oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of propagation. In a longitudinal wave, oscillations are parallel to it. Sound in air is longitudinal, with compressions and rarefactions; the air does not follow a sinusoidal path through space. A graph of longitudinal particle displacement can be sinusoidal even though particles move back and forth along the propagation direction. Classify a wave by oscillation direction, not by the shape of its graph.`],
['Wavelength, period and speed',String.raw`Wavelength is the distance between adjacent points in phase, such as successive crests. Period is the interval between successive equivalent states at one fixed position. Frequency is the reciprocal of period. During one period, the wave pattern advances one wavelength, so $v=f\lambda$. For a fixed medium under fixed conditions, wave speed may be treated as constant in the syllabus model; increasing frequency then decreases wavelength.`],
['Two kinds of graph',String.raw`A displacement-position graph is a snapshot of many particles at one time; measure wavelength on its horizontal axis. A displacement-time graph follows one particle; measure period on that axis. A graph alone must be interpreted with its axis labels. To determine a particle's motion from a translating snapshot, consider how the wave profile shifts in a short time. Do not infer particle velocity merely from the wave's propagation direction.`],
['Mechanical and electromagnetic waves',String.raw`Mechanical waves need a material medium and propagate through interactions between its particles. Electromagnetic waves consist of changing electric and magnetic fields and can travel through vacuum. In vacuum, all electromagnetic frequencies travel at c. Radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray and gamma radiation are one spectrum, ordered from longer to shorter wavelength and lower to higher frequency. Visible light occupies only a small wavelength range, approximately 400–700 nm.`],
['Phase, amplitude and intensity',String.raw`Points separated by one wavelength oscillate in phase; half a wavelength gives a phase difference of $\pi$. Amplitude measures the maximum displacement of the local disturbance. For otherwise comparable waves, intensity is proportional to amplitude squared. When energy from a point source spreads uniformly through a loss-free medium, intensity decreases with spherical area and hence with distance squared. Spreading reduces amplitude without necessarily changing frequency or wave speed.`]
],[[String.raw`v=f\lambda=\frac{\lambda}{T}`,'Wave propagation speed.'],[String.raw`f=\frac1T`,'Frequency is reciprocal period.'],[String.raw`\Delta\phi=2\pi\frac{\Delta x}{\lambda}`,'Useful phase relation for a monochromatic travelling wave.']],['A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 0.80 m. Find speed and period.',String.raw`The propagation speed is $v=f\lambda=50(0.80)=40\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The period is $T=1/50=0.020\,\mathrm{s}$. In one period the profile travels 0.80 m, but a particle of the medium returns to its starting phase.`],['Wave speed is not the local particle speed.','A displacement graph of sound is not a picture of a wavy air path.','Read the horizontal axis before choosing wavelength or period.'],['Describe particle motion for both wave types.','Interpret snapshots and time records.','Compare mechanical and electromagnetic waves.']);
add('C.3','Wave phenomena','Core','Use boundary conditions and superposition to explain refraction, diffraction and interference. Single-slit and grating calculations extend the topic at HL.',[
['Wavefronts, rays and reflection',String.raw`A wavefront connects points in the same phase; a ray shows propagation direction and is perpendicular to the wavefront. At reflection, the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, with both measured from the normal. A reflected wave remains in the same medium, so its frequency, speed and wavelength are unchanged in the basic model. At a boundary, part of a wave's energy may reflect while part transmits.`],
['Refraction and total internal reflection',String.raw`A wave changes wavelength and generally direction when its speed changes at a boundary. Its frequency remains fixed by the source. Snell's law relates the ray angles to refractive indices. When light enters a higher-index medium it bends toward the normal. Total internal reflection requires travel from higher to lower refractive index and incidence above the critical angle. At the critical angle the refracted ray would travel along the boundary.`],
['Diffraction',String.raw`Diffraction is spreading around an obstacle or after passing through an aperture. It is most pronounced when aperture width and wavelength are comparable. A narrow aperture spreads a wave more widely than a broad aperture relative to wavelength. Diffraction does not itself require a change in wave frequency or speed. Sketch wavefronts emerging from a gap to show spreading and maintain the same spacing if the medium remains unchanged.`],
['Superposition and coherence',String.raw`When waves overlap, resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual displacements. Constructive interference occurs when coherent in-phase sources give an integer-wavelength path difference; destructive interference occurs at a half-integer path difference. Coherent sources have equal frequency and constant phase difference. Equal amplitudes allow complete cancellation at a destructive point. Energy is redistributed across an interference pattern rather than destroyed.`],
['Young’s double slit',String.raw`For two narrow coherent slits, fringe spacing is proportional to wavelength and screen distance and inversely proportional to slit separation. The formula assumes small angles and a distant screen. Increasing slit separation brings fringes closer; changing both parameters requires considering their ratio. The central bright fringe has zero path difference. A white-light pattern has a white central fringe with coloured fringes on either side because different wavelengths have different spacings.`],
['Single slit and gratings — HL',String.raw`A rectangular single slit produces a broad central maximum bounded by first minima at small angles approximately $\lambda/b$. The central maximum is about twice the width of adjacent maxima. A double-slit pattern sits within a single-slit diffraction envelope. Many equally spaced slits give sharper principal maxima at $n\lambda=d\sin\theta$. Convert lines per unit length into slit spacing before calculating. A maximum exists only if the predicted sine is no greater than one.`]
],[[String.raw`n_1\sin\theta_1=n_2\sin\theta_2`,'Angles measured from the normal.'],[String.raw`s=\frac{\lambda D}{d}`,'Young double-slit fringe spacing at small angles.'],[String.raw`\theta\approx\frac{\lambda}{b}`,'HL: first single-slit minimum at small angle, in radians.'],[String.raw`n\lambda=d\sin\theta`,'HL: diffraction-grating principal maxima.']],['Light of wavelength 600 nm passes through slits separated by 0.30 mm onto a screen 2.0 m away. Find fringe spacing.',String.raw`Use SI units: $s=(600\times10^{-9})(2.0)/(0.30\times10^{-3})=4.0\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m}=4.0\,\mathrm{mm}$. This is adjacent bright-to-bright spacing, not the distance from one dark fringe to the next bright fringe.`],['Measure optical angles from the normal.','Frequency stays constant during refraction.','Do not confuse slit width with slit separation.'],['Sketch reflected, refracted and diffracted wavefronts.','Apply interference path differences.','HL: interpret envelopes and grating orders.']);
add('C.4','Standing waves and resonance','Core','Use superposition and boundary conditions to identify allowed modes, then explain the response of driven and damped oscillators.',[
['Formation of a standing wave',String.raw`Two waves of equal frequency, wavelength and amplitude travelling in opposite directions superpose to form a standing wave. The pattern does not travel and there is no net energy transfer along the ideal standing-wave pattern. Some positions never move, while other positions oscillate with large amplitude. A standing wave is not a travelling wave frozen in time: its amplitude varies with position and its phase relationships differ from those of a travelling wave.`],
['Nodes, antinodes and phase',String.raw`A node has zero displacement at all times; an antinode has maximum oscillation amplitude. Adjacent nodes are separated by half a wavelength, and a node to its nearest antinode is a quarter wavelength. All particles between two adjacent nodes oscillate in phase, although their amplitudes differ. Particles in neighbouring segments on opposite sides of a node are in antiphase. At a node, a phase of oscillation is not meaningfully assigned because the amplitude is zero.`],
['Boundary conditions',String.raw`A fixed string end is a displacement node and a free end is a displacement antinode. An open pipe end is a displacement antinode for air, while a closed end is a displacement node. Use displacement patterns consistently for pipes. With matching ends, the length contains an integer number of half wavelengths. With one node end and one antinode end, it contains an odd number of quarter wavelengths.`],
['Allowed harmonics',String.raw`Draw the lowest-frequency permitted pattern first and label it the first harmonic. A string fixed at both ends, or a pipe with matching end conditions, supports all integer harmonics in the ideal model. A pipe closed at one end and open at the other supports odd harmonics only. Combine each allowed wavelength with $v=f\lambda$ to find frequency. Ignore pipe end corrections unless a problem explicitly provides them.`],
['Driving and resonance',String.raw`A system oscillates freely at a natural frequency after a disturbance. A periodic external force drives oscillation at the driving frequency. Resonance is a large response when driving frequency is close to a natural frequency because energy transfer is effective over successive cycles. It has useful applications in musical instruments and frequency selection, while structural resonances can cause damage. Resonance does not imply unlimited amplitude: damping and nonlinear behaviour limit the response.`],
['Damping and response curves',String.raw`Damping removes mechanical energy from an oscillator. Light damping allows several decreasing oscillations and a high, narrow resonance peak. Greater damping lowers and broadens the peak and can shift its frequency below the undamped natural frequency. Critical damping returns a displaced system to equilibrium as quickly as possible without oscillating. Heavy damping returns it more slowly without oscillation. These descriptions concern free return, whereas a resonance curve describes the driven steady response.`]
],[[String.raw`\lambda_n=\frac{2L}{n},\qquad f_n=\frac{nv}{2L}`,'Matching displacement boundaries, n = 1, 2, 3, ...'],[String.raw`\lambda_n=\frac{4L}{n},\qquad f_n=\frac{nv}{4L}`,'One node and one antinode end, n = 1, 3, 5, ...']],['A pipe 0.85 m long is closed at one end and open at the other. With sound speed 340 m/s, find its first two allowed frequencies.',String.raw`The first harmonic has $\lambda_1=4L=3.4\,\mathrm{m}$, so $f_1=340/3.4=100\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Only odd harmonics occur, so the next allowed frequency is $3f_1=300\,\mathrm{Hz}$, not 200 Hz.`],['A node-to-antinode separation is one quarter wavelength.','Use air displacement, not pressure, for pipe boundary diagrams.','The second allowed mode in a mixed-end pipe is the third harmonic.'],['Draw allowed modes from end conditions.','Determine phase relationships between segments.','Compare resonance and damping responses.']);
add('C.5','Doppler effect','Core','Relate observed frequency shifts to relative motion. Treat sound and light with the appropriate model; quantitative moving-source and moving-observer sound are HL.',[
['What changes in the observation',String.raw`The Doppler effect is a difference between emitted and observed frequency caused by relative motion along the line joining source and observer. Approaching motion produces a higher received frequency and receding motion a lower one. Transverse relative motion does not give the ordinary low-speed radial shift. Frequency shift concerns the rate at which wavefronts are received, so a source's intrinsic oscillation frequency need not change.`],
['A moving sound source',String.raw`A moving source emits successive wavefronts from different positions. Wavefronts are closer together in front and farther apart behind. The propagation speed relative to the medium remains the sound speed, but wavelength differs on the two sides. Draw circles centred at successive source positions to reason about the pattern. At HL, for a stationary observer, choose $v-u_s$ in the denominator for approach and $v+u_s$ for recession.`],
['A moving sound observer',String.raw`For a stationary source, wavelength in the medium is unchanged. A moving observer meets wavefronts more rapidly when moving toward the source and less rapidly when moving away. At HL, modify the numerator to $v+u_o$ for approach or $v-u_o$ for recession. Source and observer motion are not interchangeable for sound because the medium defines the wave's propagation speed. The syllabus quantitative cases keep either source or observer stationary.`],
['Light and spectral shifts',String.raw`Electromagnetic waves do not require a material medium, and inertial observers all measure light's vacuum speed as c. The acoustic source formula should therefore not be applied directly to light. For radial speeds much less than c, fractional wavelength shift has magnitude approximately v/c. Recession shifts a known spectral line toward longer wavelengths, called redshift; approach gives blueshift. Match the observed line to its laboratory wavelength before calculating.`],
['Using astronomical spectra',String.raw`A collection of shifted absorption lines can identify a star's composition and radial velocity. A line's displacement measures radial motion, while its wavelength pattern identifies the element. The low-speed approximation neglects terms important near relativistic speeds. A redshift describes a wavelength increase; frequency decreases because c remains constant. When using fractional frequency and wavelength shifts, their magnitudes agree approximately but their signed changes are opposite.`],
['Applications and limits',String.raw`Doppler methods measure motion in astronomy, radar and medical ultrasound. A reflected wave can be shifted twice, once when received by the moving reflector and again when it is re-emitted or reflected toward the detector; use the model specified by the question rather than inserting a factor of two without justification. Always identify whether a provided velocity is radial, source-relative-to-medium or observer-relative-to-medium before selecting an equation.`]
],[[String.raw`\left|\frac{\Delta f}{f}\right|\approx\left|\frac{\Delta\lambda}{\lambda}\right|\approx\frac{v}{c}`,'Low-speed radial Doppler shift for light.'],[String.raw`f'=f\frac{v}{v\pm u_s}`,'HL: stationary observer; use minus for source approach.'],[String.raw`f'=f\frac{v\pm u_o}{v}`,'HL: stationary source; use plus for observer approach.']],['A stellar line has laboratory wavelength 500.0 nm and is observed at 500.5 nm. Estimate radial speed.',String.raw`The wavelength increase indicates recession. Using $v/c\approx\Delta\lambda/\lambda=0.5/500.0=0.0010$, obtain $v=3.0\times10^5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Since $v/c$ is small, the approximation is appropriate.`],['Approach must increase the observed sound frequency.','Use radial velocity for the ordinary spectral shift.','Signed frequency and wavelength changes have opposite signs.'],['Explain source and observer wavefront diagrams.','Estimate radial velocities from spectral shifts.','HL: select signs in the two sound equations.']);
add('D.1','Gravitational fields','Core','Represent gravitational interactions with fields and apply orbital dynamics. Potential, escape and detailed orbital energetics are HL extensions.',[
['Force and field strength',String.raw`Newton's gravitational force between point masses is attractive and follows an inverse-square law. For spherical masses viewed from outside, their masses may be treated as concentrated at their centres. Separation therefore means centre-to-centre distance. Gravitational field strength is force per unit test mass, with units N/kg, equivalent to m/s². It depends on the source mass and position, not the mass of the test body.`],
['Field diagrams and superposition',String.raw`Field lines point in the direction a small test mass would accelerate, toward the source. Closer lines represent stronger field. Near a planetary surface the field is approximately uniform over small height changes; far from it the radial variation matters. To combine fields, add vectors rather than magnitudes indiscriminately. Between two masses the fields oppose each other; outside them along their joining line the fields act in the same direction.`],
['Kepler and circular orbits',String.raw`Kepler's first law describes elliptical orbits with the central body at a focus. The second says equal areas are swept in equal times, so an orbiting body moves faster when closer. The third connects period squared with orbital size cubed for bodies around the same central mass. Syllabus orbit calculations use circular orbits, where gravitational force provides the centripetal resultant. A satellite is in continuous free fall rather than outside gravity.`],
['Potential and energy — HL',String.raw`Gravitational potential is potential energy per unit mass, defined as zero at infinity. It is negative near an attractive source because external work is needed to remove a mass to infinity. Potential is scalar, so contributions add algebraically. Potential energy belongs to the interacting system. The change in potential energy is $m\Delta V_g$; external work in a slow transfer equals this increase, while work by gravity has the opposite sign.`],
['Gradients and equipotentials — HL',String.raw`Field is related to the negative potential gradient. A steep potential-position graph indicates a large field magnitude; field points toward decreasing potential. Equipotential surfaces around a point mass are spheres, perpendicular to field lines. Moving along an equipotential changes no gravitational potential energy. A zero resultant field does not require zero potential, because field contributions can cancel as vectors while negative potentials still add.`],
['Escape and changing orbits — HL',String.raw`Escape speed is the speed needed to reach infinity with zero remaining speed in the ideal model. Circular orbital speed is smaller by a factor of square root two at the same radius. A circular satellite's total energy is negative; a higher circular orbit has less negative total energy but lower speed. Small atmospheric drag initially removes energy, causing an orbit to shrink; after settling into a lower circular orbit, the satellite moves faster even though its total energy is lower.`]
],[[String.raw`F=G\frac{m_1m_2}{r^2},\qquad g=\frac{GM}{r^2}`,'Point masses or exterior spherical fields.'],[String.raw`V_g=-\frac{GM}{r},\qquad E_p=-\frac{GMm}{r}`,'HL: zero at infinity.'],[String.raw`v_{\rm orbital}=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}},\qquad v_{\rm esc}=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r}}`,'HL: circular orbital and escape speeds.']],['At what factor does field strength change when distance from a planet’s centre doubles?',String.raw`Since $g=GM/r^2$, $g_2/g_1=r^2/(2r)^2=1/4$. The comparison uses centre-to-centre radius, not altitude. At HL the potential magnitude would instead fall by a factor of two because potential follows $1/r$.`],['Use radius from the centre, not height above the surface.','Negative potential is consistent with zero potential at infinity.','An orbiting satellite still experiences gravity.'],['Apply inverse-square fields and Kepler relations.','Combine fields along a line.','HL: use potential, escape speed and orbital energy.']);
add('D.2','Electric and magnetic fields','Core','Describe charge, field patterns and electrostatic forces. Electric potential and equipotential reasoning extend the topic at HL.',[
['Charge and its transfer',String.raw`Charge is conserved and occurs in integer multiples of the elementary charge for isolated ordinary bodies. Friction, contact and electrostatic induction redistribute electrons rather than create net charge. A conductor has mobile carriers; an insulator does not allow free bulk carrier motion. Grounding connects a body to a reservoir that can supply or remove electrons. Millikan's oil-drop results gave evidence that measured droplet charges came in multiples of one elementary value.`],
['Coulomb force and field',String.raw`Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Coulomb's inverse-square force applies to point charges and suitable spherical distributions. Electric field is force per unit positive test charge, so field direction is defined independently of the charge used to probe it. A negative charge experiences force opposite the field. Superpose fields vectorially. A material's permittivity changes the force for the same charges and separation; use the stated value rather than always assuming vacuum.`],
['Electric field patterns',String.raw`Lines leave positive charges and enter negative charges. They never cross and are denser where field is stronger. Between broad oppositely charged parallel plates, the central field is approximately uniform, with $E=V/d$; fringing occurs near edges. In electrostatic equilibrium, field inside conducting material is zero and the conductor is equipotential. For an isolated charged conducting sphere, the exterior field is radial and the interior field is zero.`],
['Magnetic field patterns',String.raw`Magnetic field lines form closed loops: outside a bar magnet they run from north to south, returning inside. A straight current-carrying wire produces concentric field circles whose direction follows the right-hand grip rule. A circular coil gives a dipole-like pattern, and a long air-core solenoid has a nearly uniform interior field. Field direction describes the orientation of a small compass north pole, not a force direction on every possible moving charge.`],
['Potential and work — HL',String.raw`Electric potential is energy per unit charge, with zero chosen at infinity for a point-charge field. It is positive near a positive source and negative near a negative source. Potential contributions add as scalars, while fields add as vectors. The system's potential energy is qV, so a negative test charge reverses the sign. External work in a slow transfer equals $q\Delta V$; work done by the field is its negative.`],
['Equipotentials and gradients — HL',String.raw`Electric field points toward decreasing potential and is perpendicular to equipotential surfaces. No electrostatic work is required to move a charge along an equipotential. Closely spaced equipotentials with equal voltage steps indicate stronger field. A charged conducting sphere has constant potential throughout its interior, equal to the surface potential, although its interior field is zero. Zero potential at a point does not generally imply zero electric field there.`]
],[[String.raw`F=k\frac{|q_1q_2|}{r^2},\qquad k=\frac1{4\pi\varepsilon_0}`,'Magnitude in vacuum; determine attraction or repulsion separately.'],[String.raw`E=\frac{F}{q},\qquad E=\frac{V}{d}`,'The plate relation uses a uniform field magnitude.'],[String.raw`V_e=\frac{kQ}{r},\qquad E_p=\frac{kq_1q_2}{r}`,'HL: preserve charge signs in potentials and energies.']],['A uniform electric field of 2.0 × 10⁴ N/C acts on an electron. Find force magnitude and direction.',String.raw`Magnitude is $|q|E=(1.60\times10^{-19})(2.0\times10^4)=3.2\times10^{-15}\,\mathrm{N}$. The electron is negatively charged, so its force points opposite to the field direction. Using the elementary charge magnitude alone must not erase this direction information.`],['The field direction follows a positive test charge.','A conductor can have zero internal field and non-zero potential.','Add potentials as scalars and fields as vectors.'],['Explain charging and charge quantization.','Sketch electric and magnetic fields.','HL: use potential and equipotential maps.']);
add('D.3','Motion in electromagnetic fields','Core','Combine electric and magnetic forces with mechanics to predict paths and identify charged particles.',[
['Motion in an electric field',String.raw`A uniform electric field produces constant force qE and therefore constant acceleration for a non-relativistic particle of fixed mass. The acceleration is along the field for positive charge and opposite for negative charge. Motion parallel to the plates and perpendicular to the field can remain uniform, giving a parabolic trajectory analogous to a projectile. Electric force can do work and change the particle's speed; use potential difference to calculate energy change.`],
['Magnetic force on a moving charge',String.raw`Magnetic force has magnitude $|q|vB\sin\theta$, with theta between velocity and magnetic field. It is perpendicular to both vectors, so it changes direction without doing work. A stationary charge or a charge moving parallel to the field has no magnetic force. Use a consistent right-hand rule for positive charge, then reverse the force for negative charge. A cross denotes a field into the page and a dot a field out of the page.`],
['Circular and helical paths',String.raw`For velocity perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field, magnetic force supplies the centripetal resultant and the particle follows a circle. Its radius is proportional to momentum and inversely proportional to charge magnitude and field strength. A larger radius can mean greater momentum or smaller charge. If a velocity component is parallel to the field, that component remains unchanged while the perpendicular component circles, producing a helix.`],
['Crossed fields and particle selection',String.raw`In crossed perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, a particle can pass straight through when forces oppose and have equal magnitudes. Then $qE=qvB$, giving selected speed E/B. The balance is independent of charge magnitude and mass, but the entry direction and force directions must be correct. A subsequent magnetic region can separate particles by charge-to-mass ratio when their speeds are known.`],
['Force on a current',String.raw`A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field experiences a force because its mobile charges experience magnetic forces that transfer to the conductor. The magnitude depends on field, current, length in the field and the angle between current and field. Conventional current gives the positive-charge direction for a force rule. A wire parallel to the field feels no magnetic force; use only the length actually within the field.`],
['Parallel wires and measurements',String.raw`Two parallel wires with currents in the same direction attract; opposite currents repel. Their interaction follows an inverse relationship with separation, unlike point-charge inverse-square forces. Combine a particle's acceleration through a known voltage with its magnetic orbit to infer charge-to-mass ratio. When using electronvolts, remember that an electron gains 1 eV of kinetic energy per volt of accelerating potential difference in magnitude.`]
],[[String.raw`F=|q|vB\sin\theta,\qquad F=BIL\sin\theta`,'Charge and conductor force magnitudes.'],[String.raw`r=\frac{mv}{|q|B}`,'Perpendicular non-relativistic particle motion.'],[String.raw`v=\frac{E}{B},\qquad \frac{F}{L}=\frac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi r}`,'Velocity selection and parallel-wire force per length.']],['A proton travels at 2.0 × 10⁶ m/s perpendicular to a 0.10 T field. Estimate its circular radius.',String.raw`Use $r=mv/(eB)=(1.673\times10^{-27})(2.0\times10^6)/[(1.60\times10^{-19})(0.10)]=0.21\,\mathrm{m}$. The magnetic field changes its direction but not its kinetic energy.`],['Magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity and does no work.','Reverse a positive-charge force rule for an electron.','Use the perpendicular velocity component for a helical radius.'],['Predict electric and magnetic particle paths.','Apply velocity-selection balance.','Analyse wire forces and charge-to-mass measurements.']);
add('D.4','Induction','HL','Relate changing magnetic flux to induced emf and use energy conservation to determine the direction of the response.',[
['Flux and flux linkage',String.raw`Magnetic flux through a flat surface is field strength multiplied by the area component perpendicular to the field. In $\Phi=BA\cos\theta$, theta is the angle between the field and the surface normal, not the plane. Flux is maximum when the field is perpendicular to the plane and zero when parallel. Flux linkage NΦ counts the flux through all N turns when each turn experiences the same flux.`],
['Faraday’s law',String.raw`An emf is induced when flux linkage changes. A change can result from changing field strength, loop area, orientation or the fraction of a loop within a field. The average emf magnitude is change in flux linkage divided by time; for an instantaneous value, interpret the rate as the slope of a tangent. A large constant flux causes no induced emf. A coil can have zero flux at an instant while its rate of change, and hence emf, is maximum.`],
['Lenz’s law and energy',String.raw`The induced current produces a magnetic effect that opposes the change responsible for it. It does not always oppose the existing field. If field into a loop is increasing, the induced field points out; if the same field is decreasing, the induced field points in. This rule follows energy conservation: an induced response that assisted the change would allow self-amplifying energy creation. An open circuit can have induced emf without a sustained current.`],
['Motional emf',String.raw`A straight conductor moving perpendicular to a magnetic field separates charge because carriers experience magnetic force. In equilibrium, an internal electric force balances the magnetic force, giving emf BvL for the stated perpendicular geometry. If the conductor forms a closed circuit, current produces a resisting magnetic force. Mechanical work to maintain the motion supplies electrical energy and ultimately heating in the circuit. Use the effective length crossing field lines.`],
['A rotating-coil generator',String.raw`A coil rotating uniformly in a uniform field has sinusoidally changing flux and hence sinusoidal emf. The flux and emf are a quarter cycle out of phase. The peak emf is proportional to field strength, coil area, number of turns and angular speed. Doubling rotation frequency doubles peak emf and doubles electrical frequency for the simple single-pair-pole model. At maximum flux, the slope is zero and induced emf is zero.`],
['Graphs and self-induction',String.raw`On a flux-time graph, steep sections imply large induced emf and horizontal sections imply zero emf. A sudden reversal in the flux slope reverses the emf sign. Current changing in a coil changes its own magnetic field, producing a self-induced emf that opposes the current change. The syllabus treats this qualitatively; a detailed inductance or RL-circuit calculation is not required. Distinguish induced emf from the current, which also depends on circuit resistance.`]
],[[String.raw`\Phi=BA\cos\theta`,'Theta is measured from the surface normal.'],[String.raw`\varepsilon=-N\frac{\Delta\Phi}{\Delta t}`,'Average induced emf; minus sign expresses Lenz’s law.'],[String.raw`\varepsilon=BvL`,'Straight conductor moving perpendicular to a uniform field.'],[String.raw`\varepsilon_0=NBA\omega`,'Useful derived peak-emf relation for uniform rotation.']],['Flux through each turn of a 200-turn coil changes from 0.0060 Wb to 0.0020 Wb in 0.10 s. Find average emf magnitude.',String.raw`The magnitude of flux change per turn is 0.0040 Wb. Thus $|\varepsilon|=N|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta t=200(0.0040)/0.10=8.0\,\mathrm{V}$. The current direction must be determined from how the field changes, using Lenz's law.`],['Use the angle to the normal in the flux formula.','Changing flux, rather than flux alone, produces emf.','Lenz’s law opposes the change, not always the original field.'],['Interpret flux and emf graphs.','Determine induced current directions.','Explain motional emf and generator behaviour.']);
add('E.1','Structure of the atom','Core','Use scattering and spectra as evidence for atomic structure. Nuclear size and the Bohr hydrogen model are HL extensions.',[
['Scattering evidence',String.raw`In the Geiger–Marsden–Rutherford experiment, most alpha particles passed through a thin metal foil with little deflection, while a small fraction scattered through large angles. This supports a mostly empty atom with positive charge and most mass concentrated in a tiny nucleus. The rare large deflections cannot be explained by a diffuse positive charge. A model is assessed through its predictions and the evidence it explains, rather than by whether it resembles a familiar picture.`],
['Nuclear notation',String.raw`The proton number Z identifies the element and counts nuclear protons. The nucleon number A counts protons plus neutrons, so neutron number is A−Z. Isotopes share Z but have different neutron numbers. An uncharged atom has Z electrons, whereas an ion has gained or lost electrons without changing its nucleus. Nuclear charge is +Ze. Keep atomic changes, which involve electrons, distinct from nuclear changes, which involve nucleons.`],
['Energy levels and spectra',String.raw`Atoms have discrete electron energy levels. An emitted photon carries the difference between a higher initial level and a lower final level. Absorption requires a photon matching an allowed energy gap. An emission spectrum contains bright lines; absorption against a continuous background removes selected wavelengths. The line pattern identifies chemical composition. A larger energy transition produces greater frequency and shorter wavelength, since photon energy is hf or hc/λ.`],
['Nuclear radius — HL',String.raw`The approximate nuclear radius grows as the cube root of nucleon number. Volume therefore grows approximately in proportion to A, while mass also grows in proportion to A, giving roughly constant nuclear density. R₀ is a scale factor, not the radius of every nucleus. High-energy scattering can probe shorter distances and reveal deviations from the simplest point-nucleus Rutherford prediction. The structure inferred depends on the resolving power and interaction being used.`],
['Closest approach — HL',String.raw`For head-on electrostatic repulsion, an incoming charged projectile slows until its kinetic energy is converted to electric potential energy at closest approach. Equate initial kinetic energy with the increase in Coulomb potential energy, assuming the target is effectively fixed and nuclear interactions remain negligible. Larger initial energy permits a smaller closest distance. This is a turning-point argument; using force alone as if it remained constant gives the wrong result.`],
['Bohr hydrogen model — HL',String.raw`The Bohr model restricts hydrogen electron angular momentum to integer multiples of h/2π and assigns discrete negative energies. Zero energy is a free electron at infinity. The ground state is the most negative level, and ionization requires enough energy to reach zero. The model accounts for hydrogen's line energies but does not provide a complete description of multi-electron atoms or modern quantum behaviour. Use energy differences, not the absolute value of one level, for spectral transitions.`]
],[[String.raw`E=hf=\frac{hc}{\lambda}`,'Photon energy equals the transition energy magnitude.'],[String.raw`R=R_0A^{\frac13}`,'HL: approximate nuclear radius.'],[String.raw`E_n=-\frac{13.6}{n^2}\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad mvr=\frac{nh}{2\pi}`,'HL: Bohr hydrogen levels and angular momentum.']],['In hydrogen, an electron drops from n = 3 to n = 2. Find the photon energy.',String.raw`At HL, $E_3=-13.6/9=-1.51\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $E_2=-13.6/4=-3.40\,\mathrm{eV}$. The emitted photon has energy $E_3-E_2=1.89\,\mathrm{eV}$. The electron's energy decreases, while the photon energy is positive.`],['A is nucleon number, not neutron number.','Atomic lines correspond to energy differences.','Negative bound-state energy is relative to a free electron at infinity.'],['Connect scattering observations with the nuclear model.','Interpret nuclear notation and spectra.','HL: calculate radii, closest approach and hydrogen transitions.']);
add('E.2','Quantum physics','HL','Use photoelectric emission, diffraction and Compton scattering to connect wave and particle models.',[
['Photoelectric observations',String.raw`When light shines on a metal, electrons can be emitted if the light frequency exceeds a threshold. Above threshold, increasing intensity increases the emission rate, but the maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency rather than intensity. Emission occurs without the accumulation delay expected in a simple classical-wave energy-sharing picture. Below threshold, greater intensity alone does not cause the ordinary photoelectric effect. These observations motivate light energy arriving in individual photons.`],
['Einstein’s equation',String.raw`One photon transfers energy hf to one electron in the basic model. The work function is the minimum energy needed for an electron to escape the surface. The remaining energy is the maximum possible kinetic energy, because electrons can lose additional energy before escaping. At threshold the maximum kinetic energy is zero. A plot of maximum kinetic energy against frequency has gradient h, intercept −Φ and frequency-axis intercept Φ/h.`],
['Stopping potential',String.raw`A retarding electric potential can stop even the fastest emitted electrons. Its energy barrier is $eV_s$, equal to the maximum kinetic energy in magnitude. Reversing or increasing the collecting voltage changes the measured current without changing the intrinsic maximum kinetic energy set by the incident frequency. At sufficiently positive collecting voltage, all emitted electrons are collected, giving saturation current. Higher intensity above threshold generally increases this saturation current.`],
['Matter waves',String.raw`Particles have a de Broglie wavelength h/p, supported by electron diffraction. Greater momentum gives shorter wavelength, so observable diffraction requires structures with an appropriate scale. A beam accelerated through a known potential has kinetic energy set by charge times potential difference; for non-relativistic particles combine this with p²/2m. Macroscopic objects have tiny wavelengths under everyday conditions, which explains why their wave effects are usually unobservable.`],
['Diffraction and duality',String.raw`Diffraction patterns from particles require amplitudes distributed over alternative paths rather than classical trajectories alone. Individual detections remain localized, while many detections build an interference or diffraction distribution. For a rectangular aperture of width b, use the de Broglie wavelength in the first-minimum relation $\theta\approx\lambda/b$ at small angles. Wave-particle duality means different experimental arrangements expose different quantum properties; an electron does not literally switch between a classical ball and a material water wave.`],
['Compton scattering',String.raw`An incident photon can scatter from an electron and transfer energy and momentum to it. The scattered photon has a longer wavelength, and the wavelength increase depends on scattering angle. There is no shift for forward scattering and maximum shift for backscattering. The result supports assigning photon momentum h/λ. Apply the electron rest mass in the Compton relation and distinguish photon scattering angle from the recoil electron's direction.`]
],[[String.raw`E_{\max}=hf-\Phi,\qquad eV_s=E_{\max}`,'Photoelectric energy balance and stopping potential.'],[String.raw`\lambda=\frac{h}{p}`,'de Broglie wavelength.'],[String.raw`\lambda_f-\lambda_i=\frac{h}{m_ec}(1-\cos\theta)`,'Compton shift for scattering by an electron.']],['Light with photon energy 5.0 eV illuminates a metal with work function 2.0 eV. Find maximum kinetic energy and stopping potential.',String.raw`Einstein's equation gives $E_{\max}=5.0-2.0=3.0\,\mathrm{eV}$. A 3.0 V retarding potential gives an electron an energy barrier of 3.0 eV, so the stopping potential magnitude is 3.0 V. Increasing intensity does not increase this value at fixed frequency.`],['Intensity controls photon arrival rate, not each photon’s energy.','Stopping potential uses the fastest emitted electrons.','Compton shift concerns the photon’s scattering angle.'],['Explain the failure of a purely classical photoelectric model.','Use energy and momentum to calculate wavelengths.','Interpret photoelectric and Compton graphs.']);
add('E.3','Radioactive decay','Core','Connect nuclear binding with stability, balance decay equations and interpret random decay statistically. Exponential decay and decay constants are HL extensions.',[
['Mass defect and binding energy',String.raw`A bound nucleus has less mass than its separated constituent protons and neutrons. This mass defect corresponds to the binding energy released during formation and required for complete separation. Binding energy per nucleon helps compare relative stability. The curve rises rapidly for light nuclei, peaks near iron and nickel, then falls gradually for heavier nuclei. Fusion of light nuclei or fission of very heavy nuclei can therefore increase binding energy per nucleon and release energy.`],
['Forces and stability',String.raw`The strong nuclear force is attractive at typical nuclear separations and overcomes electrical repulsion between nearby protons, but becomes repulsive at very short separations. Its short range means each nucleon interacts strongly mainly with near neighbours, helping explain the approximate saturation of binding energy per nucleon. Stable heavier nuclei usually need a neutron excess. A nucleus can change to a more stable state through radioactive decay; this involves its internal nuclear configuration, not an electron-shell rearrangement.`],
['Alpha, beta and gamma',String.raw`Alpha emission reduces A by four and Z by two. In beta-minus decay, a neutron becomes a proton with an electron and antineutrino emitted; A stays unchanged and Z increases by one. In beta-plus decay, a proton becomes a neutron with a positron and neutrino emitted, so Z decreases by one. Gamma emission releases a photon as an excited nucleus loses energy without changing A or Z. Conserve charge and nucleon number when balancing equations.`],
['Radiation and applications',String.raw`Alpha particles are strongly ionizing but weakly penetrating; beta particles have intermediate penetration; gamma photons are less ionizing per path length and more penetrating. Material thickness, energy and geometry affect detection and shielding. Choose an isotope's radiation and half-life for the task, such as medical tracers, thickness control or dating. Radiation emitted outside the body and radioactive material taken into the body present different exposure situations because penetration matters.`],
['Randomness, activity and half-life',String.raw`An individual nuclear decay is unpredictable and spontaneous, but a large population follows a reproducible statistical trend. Activity is decays per second, measured in becquerels; detector count rate is usually only a fraction of activity. Half-life is the interval for the number of undecayed nuclei or activity to halve. Subtract background before comparing source count rates. After n half-lives, the remaining fraction is $(1/2)^n$, not a fixed number of nuclei removed each interval.`],
['Exponential decay — HL',String.raw`The decay constant gives the fractional decay rate and relates to half-life by ln2 divided by half-life. Over a short interval, decay probability is approximately $\lambda\Delta t$ only when $\lambda\Delta t\ll1$. The exponential law allows arbitrary time intervals. A logarithmic plot of corrected activity against time has gradient −λ. The continuous beta energy spectrum indicates an additional particle carries variable energy and momentum, supporting the neutrino or antineutrino. Alpha and gamma line spectra instead show discrete nuclear energy differences.`]
],[[String.raw`E_b=\Delta mc^2`,'Use a consistent mass convention for constituents and nucleus.'],[String.raw`N=N_0e^{-\lambda t},\qquad A=\lambda N`,'HL: exponential population and activity.'],[String.raw`T_{\frac12}=\frac{\ln2}{\lambda}`,'HL: half-life and decay constant.']],['A source gives 840 counts/min initially and 240 counts/min after two half-lives. Find the constant background rate.',String.raw`Let background be b. The source contribution quarters, so $240-b=(840-b)/4$. Thus $960-4b=840-b$, giving $b=40$ counts/min. The corrected counts are 800 and 200, which have the required factor of four.`],['Correct count rates for background before applying decay ratios.','Activity is not automatically the measured detector count rate.','Beta-minus decay emits an antineutrino; beta-plus emits a neutrino.'],['Balance all four decay types.','Calculate binding energies and half-life changes.','HL: use exponentials and interpret decay spectra.']);
add('E.4','Fission','Core','Explain energy release and neutron chain reactions, then connect nuclear processes with the components of a power station.',[
['Splitting a heavy nucleus',String.raw`Fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus into two smaller nuclei, usually with additional neutrons and energy. It can occur spontaneously or after neutron absorption. A particular parent can split into several possible product combinations; do not assume one universal fission equation. Conserve total nucleon number and electric charge. The fragments are typically neutron-rich and often undergo further radioactive decays, contributing to radioactive waste and decay heat.`],
['Why energy is released',String.raw`The products are generally more tightly bound per nucleon than the original very heavy nucleus. Total rest mass decreases, and the difference appears as kinetic energy of products and radiation. Much of the initial released energy is kinetic energy of fragments, which is transferred by collisions to the surrounding material. Calculate a reaction's energy from the total reactant mass minus the total product mass, using the same atomic or nuclear mass convention throughout.`],
['Chain reactions',String.raw`Neutrons released by one fission can induce further fissions. Whether the reaction grows, remains steady or dies away depends on how many released neutrons cause subsequent fissions rather than escape or get absorbed elsewhere. A controlled steady reaction has approximately one subsequent fission per fission on average. Criticality is a balance involving geometry, composition, neutron losses and absorption, rather than simply a statement that the material is hot.`],
['Moderator and control rods',String.raw`In a thermal reactor, a moderator slows neutrons through collisions so they are more likely to induce fission in the chosen fuel. It should slow neutrons without absorbing too many. Control rods absorb neutrons and regulate the chain reaction; inserting them more deeply reduces the number available for subsequent fissions. These components have distinct roles. Different reactor designs use different arrangements, so apply the description given in the question.`],
['From nuclear energy to electricity',String.raw`A coolant carries thermal energy away from the reactor core. A heat exchanger transfers it to another fluid or circuit, often producing steam to drive a turbine connected to a generator. Shielding reduces exposure to radiation; containment also limits release of radioactive material. The nuclear reaction does not directly push electrons around the external supply circuit. Efficiency is limited by thermal conversion and mechanical and electrical losses.`],
['Products, waste and rate calculations',String.raw`Spent fuel contains fission products and other radioactive materials with a range of half-lives and radiation types. Waste handling needs cooling, shielding and containment, with long-term storage chosen to reduce release to the environment. Even after a chain reaction stops, radioactive products can continue releasing heat. To find fission rate, divide required nuclear power by energy released per fission; convert MeV to joules and account for plant efficiency before using electrical output power.`]
],[[String.raw`Q=(m_{\rm reactants}-m_{\rm products})c^2`,'Reaction energy release for a positive mass difference.'],[String.raw`\dot N=\frac{P_{\rm nuclear}}{E_{\rm fission}},\qquad P_{\rm electrical}=\eta P_{\rm nuclear}`,'Useful rate and efficiency relations.']],['A reactor releases 200 MeV per fission and produces 1.0 GW of thermal power. Estimate fissions per second.',String.raw`Each fission releases $200\times10^6(1.60\times10^{-19})=3.2\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{J}$. The rate is $1.0\times10^9/(3.2\times10^{-11})=3.1\times10^{19}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. If 1.0 GW were electrical output, the nuclear power would first need dividing by efficiency.`],['Moderators slow neutrons; control rods absorb them.','Include emitted neutrons when balancing nucleon number.','Stopping the chain reaction does not instantly remove decay heat.'],['Explain fission and chain-reaction control.','Connect reactor components with their physical roles.','Calculate reaction energy and fission rates.']);
add('E.5','Fusion and stars','Core','Use nuclear energy, radiation and distance measurements to interpret stars and their evolution.',[
['Fusion and stellar conditions',String.raw`Fusion joins light nuclei to make heavier nuclei. It releases energy when the products have greater binding energy per nucleon and lower total rest mass. Positively charged nuclei repel, so high temperatures provide large particle energies and high densities increase encounter rates. Quantum effects also allow reactions below a purely classical barrier estimate. Stars provide the necessary conditions in their cores. Balance nucleon number and charge and include all products when calculating reaction energy.`],
['Stellar equilibrium',String.raw`A stable star balances inward gravitational forces with outward pressure effects, including radiation pressure in the syllabus model. Fusion replenishes energy radiated away. If this balance changes, the star's structure evolves. Equilibrium does not mean that nuclear processes or energy transfer stop. Higher-mass stars have hotter, more compressed cores and consume their fuel at a much higher rate, so a larger fuel supply does not imply a longer main-sequence lifetime.`],
['Evolution and initial mass',String.raw`A star forms from gravitational collapse of a gas and dust cloud. Once core hydrogen fusion becomes established, it occupies the main sequence. Later evolution depends strongly on initial mass. Lower- and intermediate-mass stars can expand into red giants and leave white dwarfs after outer layers are lost. Massive stars can form supergiants, undergo a supernova and leave a neutron star or black hole, depending on the remnant. Detailed paths depend on mass and composition.`],
['Reading an HR diagram',String.raw`An HR diagram plots luminosity vertically and surface temperature horizontally, with temperature conventionally decreasing to the right. Main-sequence stars form a broad diagonal band from hot luminous stars to cool faint stars. Red giants are cool and luminous, requiring large radii. White dwarfs are hot but faint, requiring small radii. The instability strip is a region where some stars pulsate. A star's position is a state description, not automatically its motion along a single universal evolutionary track.`],
['Spectra and stellar radius',String.raw`Wien's law gives surface temperature from the black-body peak, while spectral lines reveal composition. Luminosity and temperature together determine radius through the Stefan–Boltzmann law with spherical area. For a fixed temperature, higher luminosity means greater radius. For a fixed luminosity, a hotter star has smaller radius. Ratio calculations relative to the Sun often avoid large numbers and make these dependencies easier to see.`],
['Parallax, brightness and distance',String.raw`Stellar parallax is the apparent angular shift caused by observing from different positions in Earth's orbit. The parallax angle is half the full six-month angular shift. In parsecs, distance is the reciprocal of parallax in arcseconds. Small parallax means large distance. Apparent brightness then connects distance with luminosity through inverse-square spreading. Convert consistently among parsecs, light years and metres, and account for the model assumption that absorption between star and observer is negligible.`]
],[[String.raw`d\,(\mathrm{pc})=\frac1{p\,(\mathrm{arcsec})}`,'Parallax angle is half the full six-month shift.'],[String.raw`L=4\pi R^2\sigma T^4,\qquad b=\frac{L}{4\pi d^2}`,'Spherical black-body star and isotropic spreading.'],[String.raw`\frac{L}{L_\odot}=\left(\frac{R}{R_\odot}\right)^2\left(\frac{T}{T_\odot}\right)^4`,'Useful radius and luminosity comparison.']],['A star has luminosity 16 times the Sun’s and the same surface temperature. Find its radius relative to the Sun.',String.raw`At equal temperature, $L\propto R^2$. Hence $R/R_\odot=\sqrt{16}=4$. The larger area accounts for the larger luminosity; its spectrum need not peak at a different wavelength because temperature is unchanged.`],['HR temperature usually decreases toward the right.','Parallax is half the full six-month angular shift.','Massive stars generally consume fuel faster and live shorter main-sequence lives.'],['Explain stellar energy and equilibrium.','Read HR positions and evolutionary possibilities.','Use parallax, spectra and luminosity to determine stellar properties.']);
globalThis.TOPICS=topics;
})();
